.TH afirewall 8 "17 Aug 2026" "20260817.2.0" "afirewall manual"

.SH NAME
afirewall \- A pure NetFilter Firewall wrapper for Linux

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B
afirewall 
.RB {| OPTIONS |}
.RB {| COMMAND |}

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
afirewall uses netfilter to deny by default all inbound and outbound network traffic, except what is specifically allowed.  By default, the firewall allows inbound IPV4 SSH, and outbound IPV4 SSH, DHCP, DNS, NTP, and HTTP/HTTPS traffic.  It also sets reasonable limits for ICMP traffic.  IPV4 and IPV6 traffic is configured separately.

.PP
The firewall's configuration file defaults to /etc/afirewall/afirewall.conf where individual services can be enabled.  The configuration file uses a syntax that is easy to read for human and also update / parse with Ansible.

.PP
Every service the package knows is a record in \fI/usr/share/afirewall/services.toml\fR, which
carries its ports, its limit posture and the argument for that posture.  The rules for a service,
the rule that reaches them, and the rule admitting its reply are all derived from that one record,
so a service is declared once rather than written in four places.  Records under
\fI/etc/afirewall/services.toml\fR are MERGED over the shipped ones by direction and name, so
adding a service of your own does not stop the package's list arriving with upgrades.

.PP
Templates and lists are shipped in \fI/usr/share/afirewall/templates\fR and
\fI/usr/share/afirewall/lists\fR and are replaced on upgrade without asking.  A file of the same
name under \fI/etc/afirewall\fR takes precedence.  A service template is now the exception rather
than the rule: it is for a shape a record cannot say, and the package ships two.  Please suggest
services you would like added by opening an issue.

.PP
The generated ruleset is written to \fI/var/lib/afirewall\fR.  It is derived from the configuration
rather than being configuration itself, and it persists, because restoring a saved ruleset at boot is
what \fBnetfilter\-persistent\fR(8) exists to do.  Rebuilding it at boot cannot work: the plugin runs
before the network is configured, and the external interface is found by routing lookup unless it is
named in \fIinterfaces.conf\fR.  A saved ruleset names the interface's address, so a host that boots on
a new one restores rules describing the old; \fBregenerate\fR corrects that once the network is up.

.SH OPTIONS
.OP "restore, start"
load the saved ruleset from \fI/var/lib/afirewall\fR without rebuilding it.  This is what runs at boot.
\fBstart\fR is \fBnetfilter\-persistent\fR(8)'s name for it and cannot be renamed - it runs its plugins
with \fBrun\-parts \-a\fR and sends only \fBstart\fR, \fBsave\fR and \fBflush\fR, mapping its own
\fBreload\fR and \fBrestart\fR onto \fBstart\fR as well.  If nothing has been saved this refuses and
changes nothing, rather than rebuilding: the only safe thing to do with a ruleset you cannot replace is
leave it alone.

.OP "regenerate, restart, reload, force-reload"
rebuild the ruleset from the configuration, then load it.  Run this after editing
\fIafirewall.conf\fR.  It needs the network to be up unless the interface is named in
\fIinterfaces.conf\fR.  If no external interface is found in any address family it refuses and leaves
whatever is loaded alone.

.OP "save"
rebuild the ruleset and write it down without loading it

.OP "stop, flush"
stop the firewall restoring the netfilter default (which is probably to allow all traffic).  This
deletes exactly the tables afirewall owns - the inbound and outbound pair in each family, and the
forward pair where something forwarded is enabled - and never flushes the ruleset, so rules another
tool put there are left alone

.OP "counters"
print what the sanity chains have stopped, both address families side by side.  Reads the kernel and
changes nothing, so it needs root and needs no configuration - a host whose \fI/etc/afirewall\fR is
gone can still be asked what its firewall has refused.  A \fB-\fR means that counter is not loaded,
which is not a zero.  \fBA ZERO IS NOT A CLEAN BILL OF HEALTH\fR: it means nothing of that kind
arrived, or the rule cannot be reached, or something upstream dropped it first, and nothing on the
host distinguishes them.

.OP "\-\-json"
counters: one JSON object on standard output instead of the table - \fB{"counters": [{"direction":
"inbound", "family": "ipv4", "name": ..., "label": ..., "packets": N, "bytes": N}, ...]}\fR - for
something that consumes the numbers.  A counter that is not loaded has no record, which is the same
distinction the table draws with \fB-\fR.  \fBenable\fR and \fBdisable\fR print JSON always and
do not take this flag; a configuration manager must not have to ask for it.

.OP "test"
tests the syntax of all the files and availability of network interfaces but will not enable the firewall

.OP "enable FLAG, disable FLAG"
set one flag in the configuration and report what actually happened, as one JSON object on standard
output: \fB{"changed": true, "flag": "inbound.smtp", "was": "disable", "now": "enable"}\fR.  A flag
nothing declares is refused, so a misspelling is an error rather than a line that persists, survives
every reload and governs nothing.  The exit status means success and nothing else - \fIchanged\fR is
in the output, so \fBafirewall enable x && ...\fR still works on a host that is already configured.
These edit the configuration only; NOTHING REACHES THE KERNEL UNTIL \fBreload\fR.  Neither needs
root.

.OP "\-\-dry-run"
enable/disable: do everything except the write, validation included, and report what would have
changed.  This is what a configuration manager's check mode should call, with the task allowed to
run - a command that is skipped returns success it did not earn.

.OP "add-service NAME"
declares a service the package does not ship, as a record in \fI/etc/afirewall/services.toml\fR, and
adds its key to the configuration file disabled.  Requires \-\-inbound, \-\-outbound or
\-\-forward, at least one \-\-tcp or \-\-udp port, and \-\-posture with \-\-because.  Needs no
root: it writes a record and never touches the kernel.

.OP "\-\-inbound, \-\-outbound, \-\-forward"
add-service: whether the host answers on these ports, reaches out on them, or forwards them
somewhere else

.OP "\-\-to ADDRESS"
add-service: where a forwarded service actually runs.  Required with \-\-forward and meaningless
without it.  THE FIRST FORWARDED SERVICE YOU ENABLE IS A ONE-WAY DOOR: until then this host has no
chain at the forward hook and passes everything it routes, and afterwards a chain exists there with
\fBpolicy drop\fR, so anything else being forwarded - a container runtime's published ports above
all - is refused unless it is declared too.  \fBenable\fR says so when you cross it.  The
destination must be one this host can already route to; a private range reached by translation
needs a \fBnat\fR table, which this package does not emit.

.OP "\-\-tcp PORT, \-\-udp PORT"
add-service: a port the service uses.  Repeatable, and both may be given, because a service is not always one port

.OP "\-\-posture {enforce,instrument,none}"
add-service: what a limit does with traffic over it.  \fBenforce\fR refuses the excess, \fBinstrument\fR counts it and admits it, \fBnone\fR writes no limit.  There is no default: a rule whose posture nobody chose reads exactly like one somebody argued for

.OP "\-\-because TEXT"
add-service: why that posture.  Recorded beside the rule so the next reader inherits an argument rather than a habit, and required for the same reason

.OP "-nft"
the full path to nft, default /usr/sbin/nft

.OP "-ip"
the full path to ip, default /usr/bin/ip

.OP "-ipv4dest"
address used to find the external IPv4 device and source address, default 192.0.2.1.  This is a
routing table lookup and NO PACKET IS SENT.  The default is an RFC 5737 documentation address
because it names no service: a host that carries a specific route for a real one - a split tunnel
forcing DNS down a VPN, say - would otherwise have the firewall discover the tunnel's interface and
qualify its anti-spoofing rules against it.  Override where the default route is not the answer.

.OP "-ipv6dest"
address used to find the external IPv6 device and source address, default 2001:db8::1, an RFC 3849
documentation address, for the reason above

.OP "-b, --basedir"
path to the configuration file, templates, lists, and output of nft, default /etc/afirewall

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nft (8),
.BR nftables (8),
.BR netfilter-persistent (8)
.PP
The configuration file is /etc/afirewall/afirewall.conf and documents itself in its own comments.

.SH BUGS
